<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>JSON Service</h1>
<com:SinceVersion Version="3.1" />
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.Services.TJsonService" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TJsonService</tt> answers a request with JSON. The service holds a set of responses.
Each response has an ID and a class that extends <tt>TJsonResponse</tt>. The service
parameter in the URL names the response, so <tt>index.php?json=get_article</tt> runs the
response registered as <tt>get_article</tt>. No page takes part in the request.
</p>

<h2>Configuration</h2>
<p class="block-content">
The service is declared in the <a href="?page=Configurations.AppConfig">application
configuration</a>. Each <tt>&lt;json&gt;</tt> element names a response by its <tt>id</tt>
and its <tt>class</tt>. Every other attribute is set as a property of the response object
before the response runs.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="xml" CssClass="source block-content">
<services>
  <service id="json" class="Prado\Web\Services\TJsonService">
    <json id="get_article" class="Application.Json.ArticleResponse" Limit="5" />
  </service>
</services>
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">The same configuration in the PHP format:</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
'services' => [
    'json' => [
        'class' => 'Prado\Web\Services\TJsonService',
        'json' => [
            'get_article' => [
                'class' => 'Application.Json.ArticleResponse',
                'properties' => ['Limit' => '5'],
            ],
        ],
    ],
],
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
In the PHP form the responses sit under a <tt>json</tt> key of the service entry, and the
initial values of a response sit under its <tt>properties</tt> key. The service reads the
<tt>json</tt> key only, so a response placed directly in the service entry is not
registered.
</p>

<h2>Writing a response</h2>
<p class="block-content">
A response class extends <tt>TJsonResponse</tt> and implements <tt>getJsonContent</tt>,
which returns the value to send. The service sets the configured properties, calls
<tt>init</tt> with the response's own configuration, and then calls
<tt>getJsonContent</tt>.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="php" CssClass="source block-content">
use Prado\Web\Services\TJsonResponse;

class ArticleResponse extends TJsonResponse
{
    private $_limit = 10;

    public function getLimit()
    {
        return $this->_limit;
    }

    public function setLimit($value)
    {
        $this->_limit = (int) $value;
    }

    public function getJsonContent()
    {
        return [
            'limit' => $this->getLimit(),
            'articles' => [
                ['id' => 1, 'title' => 'Hello world'],
            ],
        ];
    }
}
</com:TTextHighlighter>
<p class="block-content">
The service encodes the returned value with <tt>TJavaScript::jsonEncode</tt> and sends it
with the <tt>application/json</tt> content type in UTF-8. A response that returns
<tt>null</tt> sends nothing. A request that names a response the service does not know
receives a 404 error.
</p>

</com:TContent>
